<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dayan Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1464–<a href="/years/1517">1517</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Batu Möngke</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dayan Khan, Batu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a>, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a> khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a> who reunited the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in the late 15th and early 16th centuries, with the help of his queen Mandukhai. He defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a> and rebellious chiefs and divided the Mongols into six tümen ruled by his sons. His descendants founded the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> khanates and the Tümed realm of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altan-khan">Altan Khan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dayan-khan.jpg" alt="Dayan Khan" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Avishay · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BATMUNKH_DAYAN_KHAN.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
